.SH DESCRIPTION
A graphics driver is a user-space file server on the hardware
that
.IR gpu (3)
hands out.
It posts
.BI /srv/ srvname
(default
.BR gpu.0 )
and mounts itself after
.BR /dev ,
so its files appear as
.BR /dev/gpu/0 .
Control is text; data moves through shared segments, never through
the file server.
Every driver serves the same tree; what a family does not have is
missing.
.PP
.I Virtiogpu
drives virtio-gpu (QEMU's
.BR virtio-gpu-pci ,
.BR virtio-gpu-gl-pci ).
It finds the device's registers in the configuration space, keeps
its virtqueue in a fixed
.IR segment (3),
makes the screen the framebuffer of
.IR gpu (3)
and copies what changes to the host.
.B -p
polls instead of using the interrupt,
.B -m
sets the first mode (default: what the host asks for),
.B -d
writes debugging output to standard error (not to the console:
that draws on the screen, which makes more output).
.SS Display
.TF outputs/conn/modes
.TP
.B ctl
reads the current mode;
.BI "mode " w x h
sets it.
.TP
.B status
the device, its features, the queue, the mode, the framebuffer,
the host visible memory, a context's resource on the display
.RB ( scanout
below) and every context with its fences.
.TP
.BI outputs/ conn /modes
the modes, one
.IB w x h
per line, the preferred first.
.TP
.BI outputs/ conn /mode
reads or sets the mode of the output; the screen
.RI ( vga (3)
type
.BR gpu )
follows it.
.TP
.BI outputs/ conn /edid
the output's EDID, binary.
.PD
.SS Rendering
Present when the device can render (virgl or context init).
.TF caps/id
.TP
.BI caps/ id
the capability set
.I id
(1 virgl, 2 virgl2, 4 venus, ...) as the device describes it,
binary, latest version.
.TP
.B clone
opening it creates a context and returns its
.B ctl
file, as with
.IR ip (3)'s
clone files.
The context lives while any of its files is open.
.PD
.PP
Reading a context's
.B ctl
returns the reply to the last request, first
.IP
.BI ctx " n " seg " name va size"
.PP
the segment that holds the context's command buffers: the client
.IR segattach es
.I name
and writes commands into it.
Requests:
.TF "blob \fImem flags id size\fP"
.TP
.BI "init " "capset \fR[\fPname\fR]"
create the context on the device for capability set
.I capset
(0: the device's default, virgl);
reply
.BI ctx " n " capset " capset" .
.TP
.BI blob " mem flags id size
create a blob resource:
.I mem
is 1 for guest memory, 2 for host memory, 3 for both,
.I flags
1 mappable, 2 shareable, 4 cross device,
.I id
the renderer's object (0: plain shared memory).
Reply
.BI res " r size"
and, for guest memory,
.BI seg " name va" ,
the segment holding it.
.TP
.BI map " r
map host memory into the device's host visible bar; reply
.BI map " r seg barsize offset size info" :
attach
.I seg
.RI ( barsize
bytes) and use
.I size
bytes at
.IR offset .
.I Info
is how the host maps the memory (virtio-gpu's map_info: 1 cached, 2
uncached, 3 write-combining), and
.I seg
the bar's segment of that kind
.RI ( gpu (3)),
so that the processor sees the memory as the host does.
.TP
.BI unmap " r
.TP
.BI unref " r
.TP
.BI attach " r
.TP
.BI detach " r
unmap, release, attach to or detach from the context.
.TP
.BI export " r
give blob
.I r
a name other contexts can import it by; reply
.BI export " token" ,
128 random bits in hex, the same for every export of
.IR r .
Whoever knows the token can import the blob: pass it only to the
process meant to have it.
.TP
.BI import " token
the exported blob in this context, of any process (the context must
be initialized); reply
.BI res " r size"
as for
.BR blob .
The blob lives until the last context holding it releases it
.RB ( unref
or closing the context); the token goes with it.
Mesa's venus driver uses them for Vulkan's external memory (dma-buf
handles, VK_KHR_external_memory_fd): in a process a token takes the shape
of a file descriptor (plan9/lib/dmabuf.c), between processes it goes as
text.
.TP
.BI scanout " r w h stride
show host blob
.I r
on the display instead of the
.IR draw (3)
screen: linear x8r8g8b8 pixels,
.I stride
bytes a row,
.IR w × h
the mode's size; again for every new picture.
.B "scanout 0" ,
a mode change, releasing
.I r
or closing the context shows the screen again.
One context at a time.
.PD
.PP
Writing
.IB "offset length " \fR[\fPring\fR]\fP
to
.B submit
submits that part of the command segment on a timeline
.RI ( ring ,
0 to 63; 0 is the context's CPU timeline); the
.IR k th
submission of a context completes fence
.IR k .
The client must not overwrite it before.
Rings complete in any order; fences of one ring in order.
.PP
Reading
.B fence
returns
.IB "done submitted" :
all fences up to
.I done
are complete.
After writing
.BI "wait " k
(or just
.IR k ),
reads block until fence
.I k
is complete and return
.IB k " done" ;
after
.BI "query " k
they return at once,
.IB k " done"
or
.IB k " busy" .
.B failed
follows if the device rejected a submission.
At most 64 fences of a context can be outstanding.
.SS "Words every driver takes"
A context's requests are text in every driver; where drivers do the same
they take the same word (older words stay as synonyms):
.TF "\fLexport\fP, \fLimport\fP"
.TP
.B close
lets go of an object (virtiogpu's
.BR unref ,
i915's and intelgpu's
.BR free ,
amdgpu's GEM objects)
.TP
.BR map ", " unmap
the processor's view of an object, a segment (virtiogpu, amdgpu; i915
and intelgpu give it when the object is made)
.TP
.B scanout
a context's picture on the display, 0 the screen again
.TP
.BR export ", " import
an object between contexts by token (virtiogpu, amdgpu)
.TP
.B submit
the file a context's work goes to;
.B fence
the file its fences are waited for and asked about
.RB ( wait ,
.BR query )
.PD
.PP
Besides, each driver has its own: virtiogpu
.BR init ,
.BR blob ,
.BR attach ;
i915 and intelgpu
.BR bo ,
.BR bind ,
.BR getparam ;
amdgpu
.BR bo ,
.BR bind ,
.BR syncexport ,
.BR syncimport ,
.BR overlay
and amdgpu's own ioctls as words
.RB ( gem ,
.BR va ,
.BR cs ,
\&...).
.SS Intel
.I Intelgpu
drives an Intel GPU of generation 8 (Broadwell) through
.IR gpu (3).
It leaves the display as the firmware or
.IR vga (8)
set it up;
.B ctl
reads the visible plane,
.IP
.BI plane " p gpuaddr stride bpp w h"
.PP
and the context files are those above with other requests.
Each context has an address space of its own, 48 bits (gen 8's
four-level PPGTT; with
.B -3
4 GB in three levels); with
.B -G
all share the global GTT, without isolation.
A buffer object is a segment of pinned pages
.RB ( sticky ,
see
.IR segment (3);
not contiguous) mapped
into it;
.TF "\fLbo\fP \fIsize"
.TP
.BI bo " size \fR[\fPgpuaddr\fR]
creates one, reply
.BI bo " id seg va size gpuaddr" :
the client
.IR segattach es
.I seg
and writes the GPU addresses of its objects into its batches itself.
The driver chooses the address, or the client does
.RI ( gpuaddr ,
page aligned and free;
0 maps the object nowhere), as Mesa's drivers do with softpin.
.TP
.BI bind " id gpuaddr
moves the object to another address (0: nowhere), once the context's
work is done.
.TP
.B map plane
maps the visible plane into the context (uncached, for the display),
reply
.BI plane " id gpuaddr stride bpp w h" ;
.B free
unmaps it.
.TP
.BI free " id
frees it once the context's work is done.
.PD
.PP
Writing
.IB "bo offset " \fR[\fPrcs\fR|\fPbcs\fR]\fP
to
.B submit
starts the batch buffer at
.I offset
in object
.I bo
on the render (default) or blitter engine; it ends with
.BR MI_BATCH_BUFFER_END .
The fences are as above; one that has not completed after 5
seconds is given up and the context marked
.BR failed .
The engines run through execlists, as Linux's i915 has it on gen 8:
each context has a logical ring context (an image of its engine
registers, its page directories among them) and a ring on each
engine; an engine runs one context at a time, the others wait in its
queue, and the context status buffer says when one is done.
Before a context's first batch on the render engine the driver
writes the gen 8 workaround registers into it and runs i915's null
render state, which puts the 3D pipeline into a known state;
.B status
shows how many of those registers hold their value
.RB ( "rcs workarounds" ).
Unused entries of the address spaces point to a scratch page, as in
i915, so that stray accesses of the GPU land there.
.B -R
leaves the workarounds and the render state out,
.B -C
the clock gating workarounds at start (both to find what hangs a
machine).
.PP
The GT sleeps when idle (RC6) and its frequency follows the load (RPS,
from the GT's busy-time interrupts), as i915 has it on Broadwell; the
driver takes forcewake only around its register accesses.
.B status
shows it:
.IP
.BI "power rc6 on " ms ", " mhz " MHz (asked " mhz ", min " mhz ", rp1 " mhz ", max " mhz )
.PP
with the time spent in RC6 and the frequency running.
.B -P
leaves the power management off (forcewake held, the frequency the
firmware set).
.PP
The display is read as the firmware or aux/vga set it up (not changed
yet): every output of the DDI ports, with Linux's names
.RB ( eDP-1 ,
.BR DP-1 ,
.BR HDMI-A-1 ,
\&...), under
.BI outputs/ conn :
.B edid
(over the DP AUX channel or GMBUS),
.B modes
(from the EDID, the preferred first,
.IB w x h
and the refresh rate in Hz),
.B mode
(what it shows, or
.BR off ).
.B status
lists them with their pipes and transcoders.
.PP
A context's
.B ctl
also takes
.BI scanout " id w h stride" :
object
.I id
(linear x8r8g8b8, the mode's size,
.I stride
bytes a row, a multiple of 64) becomes what the display shows, a new
picture with each request, taken at the next vertical blank; the
request returns once the display shows it (the picture before is then
free: a swapchain's FIFO, at the display's rate), until
.BR "scanout 0" ,
the object's free or the context's end; the plane is then as aux/vga
left it.
The object is mapped write-through from then on: the display reads
memory, not the shared cache.
Fences come from sequence numbers in each context's status page and
the user and context switch interrupts (MSI where the device has it,
.B -p
polls).
An engine that runs one context for 5 seconds is reset, as
.B reset
written to
.B ctl
does it.
Objects are mapped write-back in the shared last level cache, so
that processor and GPU see each other's writes;
.B -U
maps them uncached.
Batches run in their context's address space; still, the driver is
for the host owner.
.IR Intelprobe (8)
and
.B aux/inteltest
check the hardware and the driver.
.SS i915
.I I915
is Linux 7.3's i915 driver, nearly unchanged, on a layer of Linux's
kernel interfaces over 9front's
.RB ( /sys/src/linux ),
as a library of this file server: every Intel generation i915 drives.
It serves the files above as
.I intelgpu
does (so that
.I libdrm9
and Mesa need no change); a context is an open DRM file of the render
node, its objects GEM objects whose pages are an alias segment
.RI ( segment (3))
the client attaches, revoked in every process when the object is
freed;
.B submit
is i915's execbuffer2 of the bound objects, softpinned.
The display goes through KMS on the primary node:
.B outputs
with EDID, the probed modes and the mode shown;
.B scanout
shows an object (PRIME, a framebuffer, a page flip at the next
vertical blank);
.B map plane
gives the screen's object to a context.
.B error
is i915's error state (as Linux's
.BR /sys/class/drm/card0/error ):
what i915 captured when the GPU hung, the text Mesa's
.I aubinator_error_decode
reads; writing it clears it.
A context's
.B ctl
also takes
.BI getparam " n" ,
i915's GETPARAM.
.B Status
has the lines of
.IR intelgpu :
.B "rcs workarounds"
(i915's context workarounds of the render engine that hold, checked as
i915 verifies them) and
.B "power rc6"
(i915's RC6 residency and RPS frequencies).
.PP
.B -D
leaves the display to 9front (i915.disable_display),
.B -S
leaves draw(3)'s screen where it was (the firmware's plane),
.B -d
sets DRM's debug categories,
.B -v
prints debug messages,
.B -n
starts no file server.
.B -T
runs the layer's own checks (among them the machine's DMI strings and
the GPU's ACPI node) and Linux's dma-buf selftests,
.B -M
i915's mock selftests (no hardware; as
.BR i915.mock_selftests=-1 ,
.B -f
as
.BR i915.st_filter ),
.B -L
its live selftests on the GPU (as
.BR i915.live_selftests=-1 :
the probe runs them, then lets go of the device),
and exits.
.B -p
sets one of Linux's module parameters, as
.BI i915. name = value
on Linux's command line does (a bool without
.I value
is set): those of i915
.RB ( enable_guc ,
.BR enable_hangcheck ,
\&...), of the selftests
.RB ( st_filter ,
.BR st_timeout ,
.BI live_ group
to run only the named groups) and of DRM.
.I I915live
runs the live selftests with
.BR "aux/i915 -L" :
as in Linux a run stops at the first group that fails, and the next
run starts after it; a run without output for 300 seconds hangs and is
killed.  Its log is on the boot stick
.RB ( i915live.log );
groups whose tests are not ported fail.
.SS amdgpu
.I Amdgpu
is Linux 7.3's amdgpu driver on the same layer as
.IR i915 :
the Radeons of the GCN 1.2 and later families amdgpu drives (tried on
a Navi 33, RX 7600 XT, GFX 11).
It takes the first AMD device of
.IR gpu (3)
.RB ( -g
another), loads the card's firmware from
.BR /lib/firmware/amdgpu
(the files of
.BR linux-firmware ,
unpacked; amdgpu picks them by the card's IP blocks) and reads the
video BIOS from the card's ROM.
A context is an open DRM file of the render node, with a GPU address
space of its own and one amdgpu context in it; the requests are
amdgpu's ioctls, as libdrm_amdgpu makes them:
.TF "\fLbo\fP \fIsize"
.TP
.BI bo " size \fR[\fPgpuaddr\fR] [\fPvram\fR]
creates an object.
By default in GTT, system memory the GPU reaches; its pages are an
alias segment
.RI ( segment (3)),
reply
.BI bo " id seg va size gpuaddr" ,
revoked in every process when the object is freed.
With
.B vram
in the card's memory, in one piece in the part the processor sees
through BAR 0 (the first 256 MB without a resized BAR), the reply the
same:
.I seg
then shows that part of BAR 0's write-combining segment
.RI ( gpu (3)),
the object only
.RB ( phys
in
.IR segment (3)),
revoked as well when it is freed.
As for
.IR intelgpu ,
the driver chooses the GPU address, or the client does (page aligned,
above 2 MB, below 2\u47\d; 0 maps the object nowhere).
.TP
.BI bind " id gpuaddr
.TP
.BI free " id
as above.
.PD
.PP
Writing
.IB "bo offset ndw " \fR[\fPgfx\fR|\fPcompute\fR|\fPsdma\fR]\fP
to
.B submit
runs
.I ndw
dwords at
.I offset
in object
.I bo
as one IB (PM4 packets on gfx and compute, SDMA packets on sdma); the
fences are as above.
.PP
A context also takes, as text, what a process asks of amdgpu on Linux
through its ioctls (libdrm_amdgpu; Mesa's RADV through
.BR /sys/src/mesa/plan9/drm/amdgpu9.c ):
a request is words written to the context's
.B ctl
.RB ( cs
to its
.BR submit ),
numbers as in C, domains and flags as names joined by commas or as
numbers; the reply is read back on the same fid, from offset 0 or from
where the request ended (a read after the write in
.IR rc (1)).
A request is at most one message (32 KB); the driver builds amdgpu's
structures itself, so nothing of the client's memory is read while
amdgpu works on it.
Errors are named
.RB ( "bad request" ,
.BR "no such object" ,
.BR "timer expired" ,
.BR canceled ,
\&...).
Timeouts are absolute nanoseconds of the monotonic clock, as amdgpu's.
.TF "\fLwaitfences\fP"
.TP
.BI gem " size align domains flags
creates an object (GEM_CREATE: domains
.BR cpu ,
.BR gtt ,
.BR vram ,
\&...; flags
.BR cpuaccess ,
.BR nocpuaccess ,
.BR uswc ,
.BR cleared ,
.BR contiguous ,
.BR local ,
\&...), reply
.BI gem " handle" ;
.BI geminfo " handle
replies
.BI geminfo " handle size align domains flags" ;
.BI place " handle domains
moves it;
.BI meta " handle
and
.BI meta " handle flags tiling hex
read and set the metadata of a shared object.
.TP
.BI va " op handle addr offset size flags \fR[\fPout \fIsync point\fR] [\fPin \fIsync ...\fR]
maps
.RB ( map ,
.BR unmap ,
.BR clear ,
.BR replace )
an object in the context's GPU address space; flags
.BR r ,
.BR w ,
.BR x ,
.BR prt .
.BI idle " handle timeout
replies
.B idle done
or
.B busy
and the domain.
.TP
.BI "hwctx new" " priority flags
an amdgpu context of the context, reply
.BI hwctx " id" ;
.BI "hwctx free" " id" ,
.BI "hwctx state" " id
(its reset state),
.BI pstate " id \fR[\fPvalue\fR]" ;
.B vmid reserve
and
.BR unreserve .
.TP
.BI cs " hwctx flags items
(to
.BR submit )
a submission, reply
.BI cs " seq" ;
the items are
.BI ib " ip instance ring va bytes flags" ,
.BI fence " handle offset" ,
.BI in " sync" ,
.BI out " sync" ,
.BI twait " sync point flags" ,
.BI tsignal " sync point flags
and
.BI bo " handle priority
(the objects that are not
.BR local ).
.TP
.BI waitcs " hwctx ip instance ring seq timeout
replies
.B waitcs done
or
.BR busy ;
.BI waitfences " all\fR|\fPany timeout \fR(\fPhwctx ip instance ring seq\fR) ...
replies the status and the first signalled;
.BI fromfence " hwctx ip instance ring seq
a syncobj of the fence.
.TP
.BI syncnew " \fR[\fPsignaled\fR]
a syncobj, reply
.BI syncnew " sync" ;
.BI syncfree ,
.BI syncwait " flags timeout sync ...
(flags
.BR all ,
.BR submit ,
.BR available ;
reply the first signalled),
.BI synctwait " flags timeout \fR(\fPsync point\fR) ...\fP" ,
.BI syncreset ,
.BI syncsignal ,
.BI synctsignal " \fR(\fPsync point\fR) ...\fP" ,
.BI syncquery " flags sync ...
(reply the points) and
.BI synctransfer " src point dst point flags" :
amdgpu's syncobjs.
.TP
.BI info " query \fR[\fPargs\fR]
what the device is:
.B device
(each field of amdgpu's device information as
.IB "name value" ,
a line each),
.BI hwip " type instance" ,
.BI hwipcount " type" ,
.BI fw " type instance index" ,
.BR vramgtt ,
.BR memory ,
.BI video " type" ,
.B vmfault
the same way;
.BR accel ,
.BR timestamp ,
.BR moved ,
.BR evictions ,
.BR vramfaults ,
.BR vramusage ,
.BR visvramusage ,
.BR gttusage ,
.BI sensor " type" ,
.B maxibs
and
.BI mmr " offset count instance flags
(registers) as numbers.
.TP
.BI cap " n
DRM's capability
.I n
(GET_CAP), reply
.BI cap " n value" .
.TP
.B version
DRM's version, then the driver's name, date and description a line each.
.PD
.PP
.BI map " handle
replies the processor's view of an object the client made,
.BI map " handle seg va size" :
an alias segment of its pages (GTT) or its part of BAR 0 (VRAM);
.BI unmap " handle
ends it, revoking the segment in every process.
There are no requests with file descriptors (PRIME, syncobj fds):
objects go between contexts by token instead:
.BI export " handle
replies
.BI export " token"
(128 random bits, the same for every export of the object from the
context), and
.BI import " token
in another context, of any process, replies
.BI import " handle size" ,
the object as one of that context's own (a dma-buf between, as PRIME
on Linux).
The token is good while the exporting context lives; an imported object
stays with the importer.
.BI close " handle
lets go of an object the client made through ioctls or imported (GEM_CLOSE,
its view unmapped first).
.BI overlay " handle x y w h stride
shows the client's object (XRGB8888,
.I w
by
.IR h ,
.I stride
bytes a row, a multiple of 256) at
.I x y
of the screen on an overlay plane of the output that has that part of
the screen wholly, above the screen.
An object in VRAM the plane shows as it is (the display's own
framebuffer of it, kept for a swapchain's few images); one in GTT the
GPU copies into an object of the display's in VRAM, and it stays where
the processor reads it.
Returns when the display shows it (its next vblank); refused when no
output has the window wholly or a context's picture is on the display
.RB ( scanout ).
One client at a time;
.B overlay off
ends it, as does the end of the context or a new layout of the outputs.
.BI syncexport " handle
and
.BI syncimport " token
do the same for a syncobj (replies
.BI syncexport " token
and
.BI syncimport " handle" ):
the importer's handle is the same syncobj, whose fences both contexts
wait for and signal (Vulkan's external semaphores and fences, opaque).
Mesa's RADV exports and imports its dma-bufs so (libdrm9: the token as a
descriptor, plan9/lib/dmabuf.c).
.PP
.B Status
names the chip, its memory, temperatures, power and clocks, the
interrupt ring, each ring's fences (emitted, signalled, as the ring
wrote them) and the state of each IP block.
Writing
.B "ih process"
to the driver's
.B ctl
processes the interrupt ring once;
.B waves
writes the shader waves the GPU has (where they are, their state and
trap status) and the GPU's last page fault to the log: for hangs.
.PP
A client sees its own objects only, each through a segment the
driver revokes when the object goes.
The bars themselves (BAR 0 with all the visible VRAM and the driver's
own data, the register bar) only the hostowner may attach
.RI ( gpu (3));
the GPU's own address spaces are per context.
A client's requests have a superuser's rights (Linux's
.BR CAP_SYS_ADMIN ,
.B DRM_ROOT_ONLY
ioctls) only if it is the hostowner.
.PP
At a critical temperature amdgpu powers the machine off, as on Linux
(orderly_poweroff): the layer runs
.IR fshalt (8)
(the file servers halted, then
.BR scram ),
and the driver keeps running the card, its fans and clocks, until the
power is off.
.B -X
does that once the driver has started, to try it.
.SS "Outputs and the screen (i915, amdgpu)"
.I I915
and
.I amdgpu
make draw(3)'s screen as
.I virtiogpu
does
.RI ( vga (3)
type
.BR gpu ,
the changes copied into an object the display shows: amdgpu's in
VRAM through BAR 0, i915's in the GTT through the aperture), over
every output that is on, as one desktop: each output shows its part
of the screen, the screen is as big as they are together.
At the start every connected output is on, in its preferred mode,
side by side in the order of
.BR outputs .
Writing
.IP
.IB w x h " \fR[\fPhz\fR] [\fPat \fIx y\fR]\fP"
.PP
to an output's
.B mode
gives it one of its
.B modes
(the preferred one of that size, or the fastest, without
.IR hz )
and its place on the screen (without one where it was, or right of
the others);
.B off
turns it off.
Two outputs at the same place in the same mode show the same.
The screen changes size with them, and
.IR rio (1)
with it; it can be no bigger than the framebuffer of
.IR gpu (3)
the driver made at the start: as many monitors side by side as the
display has pipes, each of the biggest mode of those connected then
(1920x1080 at least), or less if the memory is not there.
A monitor connected later goes on in its preferred mode right of the
others, one disconnected goes off (not the last one); one turned off
by hand stays off until it is disconnected.
Reading
.B mode
gives
.IB w x h " hz " at " x y" ,
or
.BR off .
A panel (eDP, LVDS) has a
.BR backlight :
reading it gives the brightness and its maximum, writing
.I n
or
.IB n %
sets it.
A context's picture
.RB ( scanout )
as big as the screen goes to every output (a page flip each), one as
big as a mode of an output to the first such output (in that mode),
other sizes are refused (the client copies).
.B -S
leaves draw(3)'s screen where it was; then there is no layout and no
scanout.
At boot
.B monitor=i915
or
.B monitor=amdgpu
in
.IR plan9.ini (8)
(or the answer to its question) starts the driver as the screen
.RI ( screenrc ,
.BR /rc/bin/gpustart ;
.B gpuflags
are its flags), its log on the boot stick's ESP as
.IB driver boot.log ;
VESA's if it makes none within 60 seconds.
.SS "Users (i915, amdgpu)"
A request is the user's whose srv file it came through, whatever
name its attach carries (anyone who opens a srv file may send any;
nothing proves one here).
.BI /srv/ srvname
is the hostowner's (mode 0600).
The hostowner writes
.BI user " name
to the driver's
.B ctl
to post
.BI /srv/ srvname . name ,
the user's (0600); every fid of an attach through it is that user's.
The user mounts it after
.BR /dev :
.IP
.EX
mount -a /srv/gpu.0.\fIname\fP /dev
.EE
.PP
A context is its creator's: its files are the creator's alone (0600),
and so are the segments the driver makes for it (objects,
maps: their owner the creator, mode 0600).
The display is the hostowner's: only it may write the driver's
.BR ctl ,
an output's
.B mode
and
.BR backlight ,
and show a picture
.RB ( scanout ,
.BR "map plane" ).
.PP
The Linux drivers learn about the machine from its firmware as on
Linux: DMI from the SMBIOS tables
.RB ( #P/smbios ,
.IR arch (3)),
for their per-model quirks, and ACPI methods of their devices
.RB ( _DSM ,
AMD's ATIF and ATCS,
.BR _ATRM ,
.BR _DDC )
through the machine's one AML interpreter,
.IR acpi (8)'s
.B aml
file
.RB ( /srv/acpi ;
the driver starts
.I aux/acpi
at the first use if it does not run; a later
.I aux/acpi
mounts that one).  ACPI notifications do not
reach them.
.SH EXAMPLES
Switch the screen to 1024x768:
.EX
	echo 1024x768 >/dev/gpu/0/outputs/Virtual-1/mode
.EE
.PP
An external monitor right of the laptop's panel, then above it, the
panel at half brightness:
.EX
	echo 1920x1080 at 1920 0 >/dev/gpu/0/outputs/HDMI-A-1/mode
	echo 1920x1080 at 0 0 >/dev/gpu/0/outputs/HDMI-A-1/mode
	echo 1920x1080 at 0 1080 >/dev/gpu/0/outputs/eDP-1/mode
	echo 50% >/dev/gpu/0/outputs/eDP-1/backlight
.EE
.PP
A context, one submission, wait for it, in C (the source tree's
.B tests/gpu/gpuctxtest.c
does more):
.EX
	fd = open("/dev/gpu/0/clone", ORDWR);
	read(fd, buf, sizeof buf);	/* ctx 1 seg gpu0ctx1 0x40000000 0x100000 */
	cmd = segattach(0, "gpu0ctx1", nil, 0);
	fprint(fd, "init 2");
	/* commands at cmd[0..len) */
	fprint(sfd, "0 %d", len);	/* ctx/1/submit */
	fprint(ffd, "1");		/* ctx/1/fence */
	read(ffd, buf, sizeof buf);	/* 1 1 */
.EE
.SS Vulkan
Mesa's venus driver
.RB ( /sys/src/mesa ,
built by the source tree's
.BR scripts/mesa )
uses this interface as its renderer: one context with capability
set 4, its command streams through
.BR submit ,
Vulkan memory as host blobs mapped through the host visible bar,
and its timeline syncs on the fences.
.PP
Mesa's RADV drives
.IR amdgpu
through its contexts' ioctls (above), with libdrm_amdgpu's interface
over them; it is in
.B /$objtype/lib.gcc/ape/libvulkan.a
with venus and hasvk, the loader taking the first driver that finds a
device (or the one
.B $VK_DRIVER
names:
.BR radeon ).
.PP
Vulkan programs present to a window with the instance extension
.BR VK_PLAN9_surface :
.B vkCreatePlan9SurfacePLAN9
takes a
.B VkPlan9SurfaceCreateInfoPLAN9
whose
.B pWindowName
names the window's image; nil means the process's window
.RB ( /dev/winname ),
or the screen without
.IR rio (1).
The swapchain's images are the device's; at every present they are
copied into host visible memory and from there into the window through
a
.IR draw (3)
connection of the driver's own.
On the screen without
.IR rio ,
the host visible copy itself goes to the display
.RB ( scanout ),
no copy by the processor, one image more in the swapchain for the one
shown; the
.I draw
screen is not drawn meanwhile.
.B WSI_PLAN9_COPY=1
in the environment always copies.
The window's size is the surface's; when it changes, presents return
.BR VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR .
.PP
There are no shared libraries: programs link
.BR /$objtype/lib.gcc/ape/libvulkan.a ,
built from
.B /sys/src/mesa
.RB ( build.json ,
the Vulkan headers in
.BR /sys/include/ape/vulkan ),
the driver with a static loader in front of it, and APE's
.IR draw (2)
libraries
.RB ( -lvulkan " " -ldraw " " -l9 " " -lfmt " " -lutf ).
The loader keeps its dispatch tables in the first word of the
dispatchable objects, as Khronos' loader does, and has no layers.
Its functions are
.BR vkl_CreateInstance ,
etc.; the
.B vk
names are weak aliases of them, so a program that keeps its own
function pointers under those names (where it would
.I dlopen
.BR libvulkan.so.1 )
starts from
.B vkl_GetInstanceProcAddr
instead.
Khronos'
.I vkcube
and
.I vulkaninfo
are ported this way.
